# Pong Plotter — Quick Usage Guide

Pong Plotter continuously traces the route to a host and measures every hop, so you can see *where* a connection is slow or dropping packets.

## Files in this folder

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `PongPlotter-Setup.exe` | Installer. Installs for your user account only (no administrator rights). |
| `PongPlotter.exe` | Portable version. Run it from anywhere; no install needed. |

## Install

1. Double-click **PongPlotter-Setup.exe** and confirm the prompt.
2. It installs to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Pong Plotter`, adds Start menu and desktop shortcuts, and starts the app.
3. Windows SmartScreen may warn that the installer is from an unknown publisher (it is unsigned). Choose **More info → Run anyway**.

**Uninstall:** Windows Settings → Apps → *Pong Plotter* → Uninstall. You are asked whether to also delete your collected data.

**Portable use:** run `PongPlotter.exe` directly. Its data is kept in a `data` folder next to it.

Pong Plotter opens in its own window and keeps running in the **system tray** when you close it. Right-click the tray icon to open, pause all targets, or quit.

## Your first trace

1. Type a hostname or IP address (for example `8.8.8.8`) in the box at the top and press **Trace**.
2. The route appears hop by hop (up to 64 targets can be traced at once). Each row shows average, min, max and current latency, packet loss (PL%), lost packets (Err), jitter, a quality score (MOS), and a latency bar. Previously traced hosts are suggested as you type.
3. Click a row to graph that hop at the bottom. Right-click a row to hide or show it. Double-click a hop name to rename it.

### Reading the results

- Loss on **one middle hop only** is usually harmless: routers often ignore probes aimed at themselves. Look at the hops after it.
- Loss or latency that **starts at a hop and continues to the final hop** is real, and the problem is at or after that hop.
- Trouble at **hop 1 or 2** is inside your own network (Wi-Fi, router, cable). Some home routers also limit replies when several targets are traced at once, which shows as loss on hops 1–2 only; if later hops and the destination show none, it is harmless.
- **Insights** (button above the table) does this reading for you: it says where loss or latency starts, lists events such as packet-loss bursts, outages and latency spikes (adjust the sensitivity slider), and ranks the best and worst periods. Click an event to jump to it.
- **Help → Interpreting results** has short guides for each case.

### Looking back in time

- **Drag across the graph** to select a time range; the table's statistics then cover just that range (shown as a chip next to *Focus*; click ✕ to return to live).
- Use **◀ ▶** above the graph to move back and forth in time, and **Back to live** to return.
- **Focus** presets go from 60 seconds to 30 days; older data comes from the history database.

## Controls

| Control | What it does |
|---|---|
| **ICMP / TCP / UDP** | How the destination is measured. TCP and UDP need a port. Hops along the way are always found with ICMP. |
| **Interval** | How often each round of probes runs. |
| **Focus** | The time window the table's statistics cover. |
| **Pause / Clear / Re-trace route** | Pause collection, clear collected data, or discover the route again. |
| **Final hop only** | Measure just the destination (lighter traffic). |
| **Window** (above the graph) | How much history the graph shows. Windows over 6 hours come from the history database. |
| **Overlay all hops** | Draw every hop on one graph. |
| **Remote…** | Trace from another computer running Pong Plotter as an agent (see below). |

Double-click the graph to add a **comment** at that moment. Route changes are marked on the graph.

## Menus and panels

- **File** — export statistics or all samples (text, CSV, image), **export a time range** (every sample, or statistics aggregated per period of 5 minutes or more), save and reopen sample sets, import/export target lists, stored sessions, workspaces, LiveShare links, email a report.
- **Options…** (above the table) — keep the target's data, or make it **ad hoc** (discarded when closed or when Pong Plotter exits); **follow IP changes** looks a hostname up every 5 minutes and follows it if its address moves.
- **View** — light/dark theme, full screen, show/hide the graph, move the graph above or below the table, overview as tiles or table, choose columns.
- **Alerts** — rules such as "average latency over 150 ms for 60 s". Actions: notify, run a program, call a webhook, or send email. Pick a sound per rule.
- **LAN** — scan your network for devices (IP, MAC, name, services).
- **Wi-Fi** — signal strength history and nearby networks (Windows, English-language only).
- **Settings** — default interval, timeout, packet size, history length, IPv4/IPv6, start with Windows, web access, email.

### Organizing many targets

- **Summaries** (the *＋ Summary* tab): named groups of targets or single hops. While a summary tab is open, new traces are added to it. Click a column header to sort.
- **Configurations** (Settings → Manage configurations): saved sets of interval, timeout and packet size, assigned per target.
- **Stored sessions**: closing a target keeps its data; reopen it from File → Stored sessions.
- **Workspaces**: save and switch between whole sets of open targets.
- **+ Column**: add your own notes column to the table (double-click a cell to edit).

### Keyboard shortcuts

`/` focus the target box · `Space` pause/resume · `←` `→` switch target · `↑` `↓` select hop · `F11` full screen

## Email alerts

Settings → Email (SMTP): enter the server, user, password and addresses, then **Send test email**. The password is encrypted for your Windows account.

## Using it from another device

- **Settings → Web access (HTTPS):** turn it on, then open the address shown (for example `https://192.168.1.20:8767`) from another device. The browser will warn once about the self-signed certificate.
  - **Web users** (Settings → Manage web users): give each person a user name and password. *Admin* users can change everything; *viewer* users can only look. Signed-in users can change their own password. You can also sign in with the access token (leave the user name empty).
  - **LiveShare** (File → LiveShare links, or *Share…* on a summary): creates a read-only link for one target, a summary, or all targets, with an optional password and expiry date. Anyone with the link can watch but change nothing. Delete a link to revoke it.
- **Remote agent:** on the other computer run
  `PongPlotter.exe --agent --token=choose-a-secret`
  then on your main PC click **Remote…** and enter `http://<that-pc>:8766`, the token, and a host to trace. Agent traffic is plain HTTP, so use a trusted network.

## Command line

Run these while Pong Plotter is running:

```
PongPlotter.exe cli list
PongPlotter.exe cli start 8.8.8.8
PongPlotter.exe cli stats 1
PongPlotter.exe cli export 1 --out samples.csv
PongPlotter.exe cli                (shows all commands)
```

## Where your data lives

- Installed version: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Pong Plotter\data` (kept when you upgrade).
- Portable version: the `data` folder next to `PongPlotter.exe`.
- Extensions (optional custom table columns) go in `data\extensions`. Only add ones you trust.

## Troubleshooting

- **Starting it again only opens a window** — that's expected: Pong Plotter is already running in the system tray, so a second start just shows the running copy.
- **"Limit of 64 open targets reached"** — close targets you no longer need (their data stays under File → Stored sessions).
- **Per-hop times look high or noisy** — the native probe helper may not be running; open About to see the probe engine.
- **TCP/UDP trace shows no final hop** — the port may be blocked or closed; try a port the host answers on (443 for TCP, 53 for UDP).
- **Port 8765 in use** — another copy or program holds it; close it and restart.
- **Wi-Fi panel shows an error** — it needs the Windows WLAN service and an adapter.
